  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Yellow tooth (Hydnophlebia chrysorhiza) is a small, wood-decaying fungus in the Meruliaceae family characterized by its distinctive yellow to golden coloring and tooth-like or spine-like structures on its underside. This species is found throughout New England where it plays an important role in decomposing dead wood and nutrient cycling in forest ecosystems.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Golden-yellow color",
      "description": "Bright yellow to golden-yellow fruiting body that fades with age, making it distinctive among similar fungi",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Spine-like structures",
      "description": "Undersurface features small, tooth-like or spine-like projections (hydnoid structures) rather than gills or pores",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small, thin fruiting body",
      "description": "Typically forms thin, delicate, bracket-like or crust-like structures on decaying wood",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Yellow tooth grows on dead hardwood and softwood logs and branches in moist, shaded forest environments throughout New England.",
